[Thinking]
**Explaining LaTeX \rowcolor Usage**

It seems the user is asking about how to use the \rowcolor feature in LaTeX. I’ll explain its usage, focusing on the colortbl and xcolor packages, along with options and examples for tables, like alternating row colors and spanning columns. I'll mention preamble lines that are necessary and clarify that \rowcolor must be placed before row content and how to handle individual cells. I'll provide example code snippets, ensuring clarity without heavy formatting.
[/Thinking]

\rowcolor is a command provided by the colortbl package (and works with xcolor) to color an entire table row. Below are the essentials, common pitfalls, and examples.

1) Preamble
- Load xcolor with the table option and colortbl:
  \usepackage[table]{xcolor}
  \usepackage{colortbl}
  (Loading xcolor with the table option is usually enough; colortbl adds extra table tools.)

2) Basic usage
- Place \rowcolor before the row you want colored (it affects the next row):
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
  \rowcolor{yellow} Name & Age \\
  Alice & 30 \\
  \end{tabular}

- You can use predefined or mixed colors:
  \rowcolor{red!20}         % 20% red
  \rowcolor[gray]{0.9}      % light gray (0 = black, 1 = white)

3) Alternating row colors
- Use \rowcolors from xcolor to color many rows automatically:
  \rowcolors{<start row>}{<odd color>}{<even color>}
  Example:
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
  \rowcolors{2}{gray!20}{white}  % start applying on second row
  Header1 & Header2 \\
  a & 1 \\
  b & 2 \\
  c & 3 \\
  \end{tabular}

4) Multicolumn and multirow interactions
- \rowcolor must come before the row; if you use \multicolumn you may need to set the cell color for the spanned cell:
  \rowcolor{blue!10}
  \multicolumn{2}{l}{Spanned cell} \\

- For better control over cell borders and colors with \multicolumn, use \cellcolor inside the \multicolumn:
  \multicolumn{2}{>{\columncolor{blue!10}}l}{Spanned cell} \\

5) longtable and page breaks
- For longtable use, load colortbl and xcolor; use \rowcolor works but must be used carefully because rows can break across pages. Instead use \rowcolors which is more robust with longtable.
  Example:
  \usepackage{longtable}
  \rowcolors{2}{gray!10}{white}
  \begin{longtable}{ll}
  ...
  \end{longtable}

6) booktabs compatibility
- row colors work with booktabs, but don't use vertical rules (booktabs discourages them). Use \addlinespace from booktabs if you need spacing.

7) Example snippets

- Single colored row:
  \documentclass{article}
  \usepackage[table]{xcolor}
  \begin{document}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
  \rowcolor{yellow} Header1 & Header2 \\
  A & 1 \\
  B & 2 \\
  \end{tabular}
  \end{document}

- Alternating rows:
  \documentclass{article}
  \usepackage[table]{xcolor}
  \begin{document}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
  \rowcolors{2}{gray!15}{white}
  Name & Score \\
  Alice & 95 \\
  Bob & 87 \\
  Carol & 92 \\
  \end{tabular}
  \end{document}

- Multicolumn cell color:
  \documentclass{article}
  \usepackage[table]{xcolor}
  \begin{document}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
  \rowcolor{blue!10}
  \multicolumn{2}{>{\columncolor{blue!10}}l}{Spanned header} \\
  X & Y \\
  \end{tabular}
  \end{document}

8) Common pitfalls
- \rowcolor must be placed immediately before the row (or use \rowcolors).
- Cell backgrounds from \cellcolor or \columncolor may override \rowcolor for that cell.
- If using colored rules or vertical lines, colors can interact; consider \arrayrulecolor to change rule color.
- For PDFs with transparency or special color models, ensure xcolor options and driver settings are appropriate.

If you want, tell me which table environment you're using (tabular, longtable, booktabs, multirow/multicolumn) and I can give a customized example.

